# Example Wreak spec: adding a rate limiter to an existing web service.
# This shows blocks interleaved inside a single feature. The form switches
# whenever the author's thought changes from exact, to sketched, to delegated.

text:
	Add per-client rate limiting to the API. Clients are identified by API key.
	Use a token bucket, kept in memory. A single process is fine for now.

# I already know this number.
code: --language python --file api/ratelimit.py
	BUCKET_CAPACITY = 20

# I can picture the data shape exactly.
code: --language python --file api/ratelimit.py
	@dataclass
	class Bucket:
	    tokens: float
	    last_refill: float

# I know the refill logic, but not the exact lines.
pseudo-code: --language python --file api/ratelimit.py
	function take(key) -> bool:
		bucket <- buckets[key], or a full bucket stamped now
		elapsed <- now - bucket.last_refill
		bucket.tokens <- min(BUCKET_CAPACITY, bucket.tokens + elapsed * REFILL_PER_SEC)
		bucket.last_refill <- now
		if bucket.tokens < 1: return false
		bucket.tokens -= 1
		return true

# The wiring: I don't mind how it's done.
text:
	Pick a sensible REFILL_PER_SEC. Use a monotonic clock for `now`.
	Wire `take` into the existing middleware however fits the codebase.
	Rejected requests get a 429 with a Retry-After header.
